fect**: Flips D3 and D4.\n    *   **Analysis**: This flips two pieces and extends the frontier significantly to the left. It opens up the C-column for the opponent, potentially allowing them to play C1 or C2, which are advantageous edge-adjacent moves.\n\n4.  **Moves on G-row (G3, G4, G5, G6, G7)**:\n    *   **Analysis**: Playing on the G-row (the row adjacent to the edge H) is risky because it often provides the opponent with anchors to take the entire H-row edge. For example, G4 allows H5.\n\n**Reasoning:**\nThe move (B, 6) is the best choice because it maximizes safety and minimizes opponent mobility. By flipping only one piece (C6), it limits the new moves available to the opponent (a strategy known as evaporation). Furthermore, it does not give the opponent a tactical reply on the edge (like A6), whereas moves like B3 or D2 allow the opponent to gain ground on the edges or corners. It securely extends my territory on the right side of the board.\n\nEvaluation: A quiet move that minimizes the opponent\'s mobility and strengthens the right-side position without conceding edge access.\nReason: Flips only one piece (C6) limiting opponent options; unlike B3, it does not allow an immediate edge capture (A6); avoids dangerous G-row or X-square placements.\nAnswer: (B, 6)'}
